    "title": "Ocean water quality rain advisory issued for all L.A. County beaches - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Iman Palm ,\n7 hours ago\nL.A. County Public Health officials are warning residents to avoid all water contact at county beaches, especially near storm drains, creeks and rivers, due to potentially elevated bacteria levels caused by recent rainfall.\n“Ocean and bay recreational waters, especially near discharging storm drains, creeks, and rivers, can be contaminated with bacteria, chemicals, debris, trash, and other public health hazards from city streets and mountain areas after a rainfall,” a news release said.\nHealth officials warn that anyone who enters the water could become ill.\nThe advisory will remain in effect until at least Saturday, Nov. 22, at 8 a.m., though it may be extended depending on additional rainfall.\nAnother storm is set to arrive in Southern California late Wednesday, bringing widespread light to moderate rain on Thursday and Friday.\nThe National Weather Service is calling for a 10% to 20% chance of rain in Los Angeles County late Wednesday night into Thursday morning. By Thursday evening, those chances climb to over 80%.\nForecasters are not expecting as much rain from this storm as from the previous ones, but with the ground already saturated and the potential for thunderstorms, officials are concerned about mudflows across the region’s burn scars.\nInformation on beach conditions is available 24/7, 365 days a year by visiting the Health Department website .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KTLA.\n2",
